Supported external volume profiles and private validation fixtures

The current release distributes only the genuine ISO9660/El Torito boot ISO.
It does not include raw IMG, VDI or other writable hard-disk images. Optical
media are read-only; saving files requires a separate writable disk created
in the user's VM manager or an existing disk preserved from an earlier setup.
For a new dedicated primary-master virtual disk, use disks, GO A:| and the
explicit format ZERO command inside Zero. This erases and initializes that
new disk's ZeroFS area; it is not a recovery command. See VIRTUALBOX.ztext.

User-owned ext4, NTFS and Btrfs volumes may be attached at other legacy IDE
positions if they already match the supported profiles. Use disks to confirm
their letters before access; discovery order determines A: through Z:.
A physical disk may expose several primary MBR or GPT partitions. Unknown,
unsupported or corrupt volumes are reported without automatic conversion,
formatting or repair. There is no guest formatter for these three foreign
filesystems, and no general-purpose compatibility claim.

Current bounds
All three backends implement root-file read, create, save/create-or-replace
and remove, with names of at most31 ASCII bytes and contents of at most400
bytes. Directory trees, unrestricted allocation, crash recovery and many
on-disk feature combinations remain unsupported. Exact constraints are in
fs/ext4-profile.ztext, fs/ntfs-profile.ztext and fs/btrfs-profile.ztext.
- ext4: a clean single block group,4096-byte blocks, compatible inode sizes,
  filetype and optional depth-zero extents, no journal or metadata checksums.
- NTFS:3.1,4096-byte clusters, resident file data, existing MFT allocation,
  a single leaf root index, and unused formatter journal restart pages.
  Initialized Windows journals are refused even with a clean volume flag.
- Btrfs: a single device, SINGLE chunks, CRC32C, supported feature masks,
  level-zero metadata trees, inline files, and no free-space-tree.
Default modern formatter settings and arbitrary existing Windows/Linux
volumes commonly fall outside these profiles. Unsupported profiles must not
be presented as working support or silently reformatted to fit.

Examples after an existing compatible volume has been attached and identified:
  disks
  GO B:|
  save note Hello on this volume
  read note
  watch
These are command examples, not a promise that B:| exists on an ISO-only VM.
Keep the same user-owned writable disk across complete restarts and ISO
upgrades. Changing or discarding that disk loses access to its saved files.

Private test evidence
Development used disposable compatible ext4/NTFS/Btrfs images, a three-partition
MBR fixture, and a GPT ext4 fixture with the read-only attribute. Standard host
formatting and checking tools made and validated filesystem fixtures; our own
Zero fixture builder authored partition tables and CRCs. These external tools
are test infrastructure and are not part of the guest implementation.
The integrated ISO was tested against those private devices; details and
remaining limits are recorded in verification.ztext and the verification tree.
Historical VBoxManage container conversions and exact RAW roundtrips remain
private evidence. They do not make a VDI a current deliverable or establish
native VirtualBox guest execution, which was unavailable in this workspace.
Fixture scripts describe reproducible tests; generated IMG/VDI files are
excluded from the current source distribution. No existing user disk is
deleted or replaced by this distribution change.
